• Partly, Sir.

  This is the gateway-side client, plus enough protocol and test-server code to reconstruct a compatible vendor
  side. It is not OpenAI’s production vendor service.

  ## Review outcome

  Reuse the client and protocol. Do not deploy the included server.

  The repository gives us:

  - GET /v1/tunnels/{id}/poll
  - POST /v1/tunnels/{id}/response
  - Request correlation, deadlines and retries
  - MCP streaming and session termination
  - Backpressure, health, metrics and diagnostics
  - A complete in-memory server showing the other end of the conversation

  That local server is explicitly a single-process development control plane. It has no production tenancy,
  persistence, HA, public caller authentication or fleet management. Source
  (https://github.com/openai/tunnel-client/blob/e288bde76d811e4fc5e2ca076775eab04459c682/pkg/localproxy/localproxy.go#L1023-L1173)

  ## BOS fit

  We can replace the current BOS WebSocket transport with a pinned fork of this client and protocol.

  Keep from BOS:

  - Enrollment and RouterOS identity
  - DPAPI credential storage
  - Five-digit site IDs, mapped to separate opaque tunnel IDs
  - Public site access keys and revocation
  - FastMCP on port 7087
  - Catalogue HMAC health proof
  - Vendor TUI, audit, rate limits and body limits

  Build on the vendor side:

  - Production /poll and /response service
  - Authenticated public MCP ingress
  - Durable request queues
  - Tenant and site isolation
  - HA and failover
  - Fleet administration

  That replaces the transport described in /C:/Users/danie/building-os/host/runtime/server/VENDOR-RELAY-
  CONNECTOR.md:9, not the entire BOS vendor architecture.

  ## Security findings

  A straight drop-in is unsafe:

  - Remote forwarded headers override configured static headers. A caller’s Authorization could replace the
    private BOS bearer used by port 7087. We must strip remote authentication headers and inject the local key
    afterwards. Source

    (https://github.com/openai/tunnel-client/blob/e288bde76d811e4fc5e2ca076775eab04459c682/pkg/mcpclient/fxmodule.go#L280-L320)

  - Successful poll bodies are read without a size limit.
  - HTTPS is not strictly enforced by configuration validation.
  - Readiness does not prove a successful vendor poll.
  - Remote admin UI mode has no authentication. Keep it loopback-only.
  - Delivery is effectively at-most-once after polling. A crash after a local write but before response
    delivery can lose the result, so BOS write confirmation and idempotency remain necessary.

